	THE HOLE

The hole is one PLBG pattern, and its F R extensions are hole chords too:

	          plain       *
hole      [verb]      not [verb]
F hole    always      never
R hole    just        not just
FR hole   also        not also

*F writes "never" in place of "not", so "I never run" takes no do. "not
always" is 169 to never's 2,012 in web+news. Every verb outside the bank gets
these three adverbs in two strokes: `#TU-F<hole> RUPB` is "I've never run".

The hole takes 4 bank chords where -RG took 1, so the bank holds 59 verbs.
The three it gives up are the lowest-value verbs in the bank; by today's
ranking, bring, stop and tell.

Its complement is the article class with "to": -T the, -S a(n), -TS to. After
a verb ranked 60 or lower, the next word is "the" 11.1%, "to" 7.1%, "a" 6.2%,
"that" 2.8%.


	LINKER (undecided)

Z    have to     ZD    had to
RO   need to     ROD   needed to

On Z, "have to" composes with modality and aspect: "I'll have to", "might have
had to". It follows a modal 1,192 times in 8,538 in web+news, and in RO it
cannot. RO then holds a linker that rarely follows a modal: "need to" (11%) or
"want to" (7%). Personal writing has need to 135, want to 80; web+news has
want to 8,566, need to 6,949.

If RO is "need to", the bank's "need" is mostly "need the" and "need a", so it
takes the article class.


	* ON AN ADVERB

* on an adverb whose "not" form is rare writes its negative form instead, and
the negative form replaces "not". Only always/never is on a chord. The same rule
covers still/no longer (5 to 986), already/yet (24 to 598) and also/either (8
to 68) if they ever get one. just, really and only take "not" freely (961, 221,
1,540) and keep it.


